* START-HISTORY:
* 30 Aug 26 Windows port - Remove-CimInstance moves BEHIND the directory
*            removal and inside the "-not $dirleft" guard.  It removes both
*            halves in Windows' own order, so on a machine that could not
*            release the directory the ProfileList entry had already gone -
*            the exact split 36 exists to prevent, and the guard could not
*            undo it.  Measured on -Run b74 and b75, the first runs ever to
*            reach the arm.  PRE_RELEASE_FIXES.md 83.
* 14 Aug 26 Windows port - Remove-LocalUser replaces userdel
* 21 Aug 26 Windows port - and it takes the user PROFILE with it now
* 27 Aug 26 Windows port - and the ProfileList REGISTRY ENTRY even when
*           Remove-CimInstance fails.  PRE_RELEASE_FIXES.md 32.
* 28 Aug 26 Windows port - THE DIRECTORY FIRST, and the registry entry only if
*           the directory went.  Both halves are KEPT together on failure and
*           recorded for the boot sweep to reclaim.  PRE_RELEASE_FIXES.md 36.
* 30 Aug 26 Windows port - ps_script instead of os.execute, so the removal runs
*           through the ELEVATED HELPER.  os.execute spawns PowerShell at the SD
*           process's own privilege, so from a session that reached SDSYS by
*           LOGTO from an unelevated start Remove-LocalUser was denied and the
*           account survived.  PRE_RELEASE_FIXES.md 72.
* END-HISTORY
*
* START-DESCRIPTION:
*
*    x = !delete_user(username)
*
*    Delete a Windows local user.  Returns true on success; on failure sets
*    STATUS() and returns false.
*
*      0 success
*      1 the account could not be removed
*      2 the name is not a valid OS user name
*      5 access denied: SD is not running elevated
*      6 the account went, the profile DIRECTORY did not, so the ProfileList
*        entry was KEPT with it and the pair is recorded for reclaim - below
*      7 the account and the directory went, the ProfileList REGISTRY ENTRY
*        did not - see below
*      8 as 6, and the reclaim record could NOT be written, so nothing will
*        come back for the pair on its own - see below
*
*    THE PROFILE GOES TOO, AND THIS REVERSES WHAT THIS COMMENT USED TO SAY.
*    It said the directory was left alone because "C:\Users\<name> is the
*    person's own data and removing an SD account is not a reason to destroy
*    it".  That reasoning is sound for an account SD merely BORROWED and does
*    not apply to one SD CREATED - and DELACC is the only caller, which asks
*    !is_sd_user first and never gets here for a borrowed account.
*
*    WHAT THE OLD BEHAVIOUR COST, measured 21 Aug 2026: TWENTY-SIX orphaned
*    profiles under C:\Users going back to 14 Aug, one per account-creating
*    verifier run.  Every one had a live ProfileList registry entry with no
*    account behind it.
*
*    AND THE REGISTRY ENTRY IS THE HALF THAT BITES.  Removing the DIRECTORY
*    alone leaves the entry, and Windows then honours it the next time an
*    account of that name appears - by putting the new profile at
*    C:\Users\<name>.<COMPUTERNAME> instead.  That is where sdacct19.GITORLI,
*    sdacct20.GITORLI, sdacct27.GITORLI and sdsshprobe.GITORLI came from.
*    Remove-CimInstance on Win32_UserProfile takes both halves; Remove-Item
*    would take the worse one.
*
*    27 Aug 26 - AND WHEN Remove-CimInstance FAILS IT TAKES NEITHER, WHICH IS
*    THE CASE THIS ROUTINE USED TO LEAVE BEHIND.  The catch below exited 6 and
*    stopped, so a profile whose hive was still loaded - the ordinary state
*    after a session - kept its registry entry, and the next account of that
*    name got the suffixed home.  MEASURED 27 Aug 2026, PRE_RELEASE_FIXES.md
*    32: C:\Users\sdsshb48 AND C:\Users\sdsshb48.GITORLI on the same host, with
*    53 stale ProfileList entries for accounts Get-LocalUser says do not exist.
*    THE SYMPTOM WAS NOT A MISSING DIRECTORY: it was ssh public-key
*    authentication refused, because sshd reads authorized_keys under the
*    profile Windows assigns and the key was under the other one.
*
*    SO THE REGISTRY ENTRY IS NOW REMOVED IN ITS OWN RIGHT, after the CIM call
*    rather than instead of it.  Remove-CimInstance is still tried first and is
*    still the right tool - it takes both halves and it does the bookkeeping -
*    but its failure no longer decides anything.
*
*    28 Aug 26 - AND THE ORDER IS NOW THE OTHER WAY ROUND, WHICH REVERSES PART
*    OF WHAT 27 Aug DID.  Owner's ruling, PRE_RELEASE_FIXES.md 36: try the
*    DIRECTORY first, and remove the ProfileList entry ONLY IF THAT SUCCEEDED.
*
*    THE REASON IS THAT THE ENTRY IS THE HANDLE, AND 27 Aug's ORDER THREW IT
*    AWAY.  Everything that cleans up a profile finds it through ProfileList:
*    Win32_UserProfile enumerates from that key, so no entry means no object,
*    and gplbld/clean-test-profiles.ps1 inherits the blindness because it
*    sweeps with Remove-CimInstance.  Removing the entry while the directory
*    is still there therefore left an anonymous folder under C:\Users that
*    NOTHING on the machine could find by profile.
*
*    MEASURED, NOT REASONED, 28 Aug 2026: C:\Users\sdapiab49, sdapiidb49 and
*    sdapinb49 were on this host with a directory and NO ProfileList entry, and
*    a predicted-in-advance sweep skipped all three exactly as predicted while
*    removing the seven orphans that still had entries.  They had to be deleted
*    by hand.  That is the cost of the old order in one sentence.
*
*    SO ON FAILURE BOTH HALVES ARE KEPT TOGETHER.  The pair stays consistent,
*    the profile stays visible to Win32_UserProfile, and the account name is
*    blocked either way - which it already was, because either half on its own
*    pushes the next account of that name to a suffixed home.  Nothing is lost
*    by keeping the entry and the only handle is kept.
*
*    AND SOMETHING COMES BACK FOR IT.  The pair is recorded under
*    C:\ProgramData\SD\profile-reclaim, one file per SID, and
*    gplbld/reclaim-profiles.ps1 sweeps that directory when the SD service
*    starts - which is every boot, as LocalSystem, by which time the previous
*    boot's hives are down and the directory can finally be deleted.  The
*    record names the DIRECTORY as well as the SID, so the sweep does not have
*    to enumerate ProfileList and cannot inherit the blindness above.
*
*    THE RECORD DIRECTORY IS CREATED WITH ITS OWN ACL AND THAT IS NOT
*    TIDINESS.  C:\ProgramData\SD grants sdusers:(OI)(CI)M, inherited by
*    everything underneath, so a reclaim store created without breaking
*    inheritance would be a list of directories that every SD user can edit and
*    that LocalSystem later deletes - a local privilege escalation of exactly
*    the shape PS_SCRIPT's header records for SDSYS\PSTMP.  Inheritance is
*    broken in the same command as the grants, SYSTEM and Administrators only.
*    gplbld/secure-reclaim.ps1 does the same thing at install time so the
*    directory already exists with the right ACL before any SD user could
*    create it first, and the sweep re-asserts it at every boot.
*
*    THE RESTART STAYS OUT OF THIS PATH.  Also the owner's ruling: the reclaim
*    rides the next boot that happens anyway.  DELETE.ACCOUNT does not ask for
*    one, does not schedule one, and says what it left behind instead.
*
*    THE SID IS READ BEFORE THE ACCOUNT IS DELETED, and the order is not
*    incidental: a profile is keyed by SID, and once the account is gone the
*    name can no longer be resolved to one.  THE PROFILE PATH IS READ BEFORE
*    THE ENTRY IS REMOVED, for the same kind of reason: the entry is the only
*    place that records where the directory is.
*
*    STATUS 6 AND 7 BOTH MEAN THE ACCOUNT WENT AND SOMETHING OF THE PROFILE
*    DID NOT.  ***NEITHER OF THEM IS HARMLESS, AND THE FIRST VERSION OF THIS
*    COMMENT SAID 6 WAS.***  It read "it costs disk and nothing else - the
*    registry entry is gone, so an account created with this name later gets
*    its proper home".  MEASURED FALSE THE SAME DAY, on the install that
*    carried the registry fix: delete.account b49home removed the entry
*    correctly, left the DIRECTORY, and the recreated account still landed at
*    C:\Users\b49home.GITORLI - because Windows will not put a new profile
*    where a directory already sits either.
*
*      6  the DIRECTORY is left, and the registry entry is left WITH it on
*         purpose.  A later account of the same name is pushed to a suffixed
*         home, exactly as 7 does it.  Recorded for reclaim; the next SD
*         service start takes both halves.
*      7  the REGISTRY ENTRY is left and the directory went.  Same consequence
*         by the other route.  Recorded too - a lone entry is the half a sweep
*         can always clear.
*      8  as 6, and NOTHING IS COMING BACK FOR IT.  The record could not be
*         written, so the pair will sit there until somebody removes it by
*         hand.  A separate status because the way out is different: 6 needs a
*         restart and nothing else, 8 needs a person.
*
*    28 Aug 26 - THE STATUSES NOW SAY WHAT WAS LEFT AND WHETHER ANYTHING WILL
*    COME BACK FOR IT, which is what the caller has to tell the user.  They
*    used to say only which half could not be removed, from a version that
*    removed the two independently.
*
*    None of the three is the same failure as 1 and the caller says so: the
*    account is deleted either way, so re-running would report "does not exist"
*    and never retry the profile.  gplbld/reclaim-profiles.ps1 is what returns
*    for 6 and 7; gplbld/clean-test-profiles.ps1 remains the development sweep.
*
*    BOTH HALVES ARE MEASURED RATHER THAN INFERRED FROM WHICH CALL THREW.  A
*    thrown Remove-CimInstance does not tell you what it managed to remove
*    first, so the script tests for each half after the attempt instead of
*    concluding from the exception.
*
* END-DESCRIPTION
*
* START-CODE

$internal
function delete_user(username)
$catalog !delete_user

deffun valid_os_name(name) calling '!valid_os_name'
deffun ps_script(script) calling '!ps_script'

   user_deleted = @false
   set.status 0

   if not(valid_os_name(username)) then
      set.status 2
      return user_deleted
   end

   ps = 'if (-not (New-Object Security.Principal.WindowsPrincipal('
   ps := '[Security.Principal.WindowsIdentity]::GetCurrent())).IsInRole('
   ps := '[Security.Principal.WindowsBuiltInRole]::Administrator)) { exit 5 }; '
   ps := '$n = "' : username : '"; '
   * The SID FIRST - see the note above.  A profile is keyed by SID and the
   * name cannot be resolved to one after the account has gone.
   ps := 'try { $sid = (Get-LocalUser -Name $n -ErrorAction Stop).SID.Value } '
   ps := 'catch { exit 1 }; '
   * The profile's registry entry, named while the SID is still in hand.
   ps := '$key = Join-Path '
   ps := '"HKLM:\SOFTWARE\Microsoft\Windows NT\CurrentVersion\ProfileList" $sid; '
   * AND ITS DIRECTORY, READ BEFORE ANYTHING IS REMOVED.  That entry is the
   * only record of where the directory is, and it is about to go.  The value
   * may be a REG_EXPAND_SZ, so it is expanded rather than trusted as written -
   * an unexpanded %SystemDrive% would make Test-Path answer false and report a
   * directory that is still there as gone.
   ps := '$dir = ""; '
   ps := 'try { $dir = (Get-ItemProperty -LiteralPath $key -Name ProfileImagePath '
   ps := '-ErrorAction Stop).ProfileImagePath } catch { }; '
   ps := 'if ($dir -ne "") { $dir = [Environment]::ExpandEnvironmentVariables($dir) }; '
   ps := 'try { Remove-LocalUser -Name $n -ErrorAction Stop } catch { exit 1 }; '
   * An account that never signed in has no profile, and that is not a
   * failure: Where-Object simply matches nothing and Remove-CimInstance is
   * handed an empty pipeline.  Special profiles are excluded on principle
   * rather than because one could match - a local account's SID never is one.
   *
   * 27 Aug 26 - THIS CATCH NO LONGER EXITS, and that one word is the fix.
   * It used to exit 6 here, which left the registry entry behind with the
   * directory - see the note above.  The failure is allowed to fall through
   * so the entry is dealt with in its own right below.
   * 30 Aug 26 - ***THE Remove-CimInstance MOVED BELOW THE DIRECTORY REMOVAL,
   * AND THAT IS PRE_RELEASE_FIXES.md 83.***  It used to run HERE, first, and it
   * removes BOTH halves in Windows' own order - so on a machine that could not
   * release the directory, the ProfileList entry had already gone by the time
   * anything tested for it.  The guard below ("the entry only if the directory
   * went") governs only SD's OWN second removal and could never undo that.
   *
   * MEASURED, NOT REASONED, ON -Run b74 AND b75 - the first runs ever to reach
   * this arm, with the profile pinned open by verify-delaccount step 6.  Two
   * independent instruments agreed: the verifier read "ProfileList entry was
   * KEPT with it: expected True, got False" straight after the verb, and
   * reclaim-profiles.log wrote "before: directory present, ProfileList entry
   * gone" four minutes later.
   *
   * SO THE COMMENT BELOW - "AND IT GOES FIRST NOW" - WAS TRUE OF THE WRONG PAIR.
   * It reordered the two EXPLICIT removals and left the call that does both
   * ahead of them, which is why the invariant read as satisfied in source and
   * was not in fact.  The directory genuinely goes first now.
   * 27 Aug 26 - AND THE DIRECTORY IN ITS OWN RIGHT TOO, for the reason
   * MEASURED an hour after the registry half was fixed: a directory left at
   * C:\Users\<name> makes Windows give the NEXT account of that name the
   * suffixed home just as surely as a stale registry entry does.  Both halves
   * cause the same symptom, so removing only one of them fixes only half the
   * defect - see the note above.
   *
   * 28 Aug 26 - AND IT GOES FIRST NOW.  The two removals used to be
   * independent and in the other order, which meant a machine that could not
   * release the directory still lost the registry entry - and the entry is the
   * only handle any sweep has.  PRE_RELEASE_FIXES.md 36.
   ps := 'if (($dir -ne "") -and (Test-Path -LiteralPath $dir)) { '
   ps := 'try { Remove-Item -LiteralPath $dir -Recurse -Force -ErrorAction Stop } catch { } }; '
   * Test for the half rather than conclude from which call threw: a thrown
   * Remove-CimInstance does not say what it removed before it threw.
   ps := '$dirleft = (($dir -ne "") -and (Test-Path -LiteralPath $dir)); '
   * 28 Aug 26 - THE ENTRY ONLY IF THE DIRECTORY WENT.  This one condition is
   * the whole of the owner's ruling: keep both halves together rather than
   * leave a folder nothing can enumerate.
   *
   * 30 Aug 26 - AND Remove-CimInstance IS NOW INSIDE IT, PRE_RELEASE_FIXES.md 83.
   *   It is still the sanctioned way to deregister a profile - clean-test-
   *   profiles.ps1 uses it for exactly that - so it is not replaced, only moved
   *   behind the test it was defeating.  Deregistering a profile whose directory
   *   has already gone is the ordinary case for it and leaves nothing dangling.
   *   The explicit Remove-Item on the key stays after it as the fallback for a
   *   CIM call that failed without throwing, which this tree has already been
   *   caught by once (PRE_RELEASE_FIXES.md 81).
   ps := 'if (-not $dirleft) { '
   ps := 'try { Get-CimInstance Win32_UserProfile | '
   ps := 'Where-Object { $_.SID -eq $sid -and -not $_.Special } | '
   ps := 'Remove-CimInstance -ErrorAction Stop } catch { }; '
   ps := 'if (Test-Path -LiteralPath $key) { '
   ps := 'try { Remove-Item -LiteralPath $key -Recurse -Force -ErrorAction Stop } catch { } } }; '
   ps := '$keyleft = (Test-Path -LiteralPath $key); '
   * 28 Aug 26 - AND RECORD WHAT WAS LEFT, so the boot sweep has something to
   * come back for.  The SID names the file and the DIRECTORY is written into
   * it: the sweep must not have to enumerate ProfileList, or it inherits the
   * blindness that left three folders on this host nothing could find.
   *
   * THE ACL IS APPLIED WHERE THE DIRECTORY IS CREATED, not left to
   * inheritance.  C:\ProgramData\SD grants sdusers Modify to everything
   * underneath, and this is a list of directories LocalSystem later deletes.
   * gplbld/secure-reclaim.ps1 does the same at install time and the sweep
   * re-asserts it at every boot; this is the third place because the delete
   * path must be safe on a tree that predates either of them.
   ps := '$rec = $false; '
   ps := 'if ($dirleft -or $keyleft) { try { '
   ps := '$pd = $env:ProgramData; if (($pd -eq $null) -or ($pd -eq "")) { $pd = "C:\ProgramData" }; '
   ps := '$rd = Join-Path $pd "SD\profile-reclaim"; '
   ps := 'if (-not (Test-Path -LiteralPath $rd)) { '
   ps := 'New-Item -ItemType Directory -Path $rd -Force -ErrorAction Stop | Out-Null; '
   ps := '& "$env:SystemRoot\System32\icacls.exe" $rd /inheritance:r '
   ps := '/grant "*S-1-5-18:(OI)(CI)F" /grant "*S-1-5-32-544:(OI)(CI)F" | Out-Null }; '
   ps := 'Set-Content -LiteralPath (Join-Path $rd $sid) -Encoding ASCII -ErrorAction Stop -Value @('
   ps := '("sid=" + $sid), ("account=" + $n), ("directory=" + $dir), '
   ps := '("recorded=" + (Get-Date -Format "yyyy-MM-dd HH:mm:ss"))); '
   ps := '$rec = $true } catch { } }; '
   * 6 and 8 differ in one thing only and it is the thing the user has to act
   * on: whether a restart is enough, or whether a person has to come back.
   ps := 'if ($dirleft) { if ($rec) { exit 6 } else { exit 8 } }; '
   ps := 'if ($keyleft) { exit 7 }; '
   ps := 'exit 0'

* 30 Aug 26 Windows port - ps_script, NOT os.execute.  PRE_RELEASE_FIXES 72.
*
*   THIS ROUTINE CHANGES WINDOWS STATE - it removes a local user, its profile
*   and its ProfileList key - and os.execute runs PowerShell DIRECTLY, at the
*   privilege of the SD process.  !ps_script routes through the elevated helper
*   when K$ADMINISTRATOR is set (PS_SCRIPT:166).  Every other routine here that
*   changes Windows state already used it: CREATE_USER:104, SET_PASSWD:130 and
*   OS_GROUP.  This one was the exception and nothing had noticed.
*
*   WHY IT MATTERED, AND NOTHING REFUSED.  After LOGTO SDSYS, CPROC:2769 sets
*   K$ADMINISTRATOR - which IS USR_ADMIN - so os_permitted() returns TRUE at
*   op_sh.c:161 and os.execute is ALLOWED.  But the PowerShell it spawns
*   inherits the SD process token, and from a session that reached SDSYS by
*   LOGTO from an unelevated start that token is not elevated, so
*   Remove-LocalUser was denied and this returned non-zero.
*
*   ITS CALLER THEN THREW THAT AWAY.  CREATEA's rollback was "void
*   delete_user(...)" followed by 10086, "An account must have a password.
*   Nothing was created." - so the account SURVIVED and the message said it had
*   not.  Measured 30 Aug 2026: the owner's "john" outlived a failed
*   create.account on the VM, in no group, unconfined, invisible to the sweep in
*   remove-sdaccounts.ps1 because that reads sdusers.
*
*   THE READ-ONLY QUERIES AROUND HERE STAY ON os.execute AND SHOULD.
*   IS_USER, IS_GROUP, IS_GRP_MEMBER, IS_SD_USER and PROFILE_DIR only ASK
*   Windows things; they need no privilege and routing them through the helper
*   would buy a round trip for nothing.  The rule this file now follows is the
*   one the tree already followed everywhere else: CHANGE state through
*   ps_script, READ state with os.execute.
   stat = ps_script(ps)
   begin case
      case stat = 0
         user_deleted = @true

      * 21 Aug 26 - THE ACCOUNT WENT AND THE PROFILE DID NOT, which is a
      * SUCCESS with a warning attached rather than a failure.  Reporting it
      * as a failure would be wrong twice: the account really is gone, and the
      * caller would offer to retry something that can never succeed now,
      * because the name no longer resolves to a SID.
      *
      * 27 Aug 26 - AND 7 IS THE SAME SHAPE FOR THE SERIOUS HALF.  Kept as a
      * separate status rather than folded into 6 because the caller's warning
      * has to differ: 6 costs disk, 7 moves a recreated account's home.
      *
      * 28 Aug 26 - AND 8 JOINS THEM.  It is 6 with nothing coming back for it,
      * and the caller's warning differs again for the same reason: a restart
      * settles 6, only a person settles 8.
      case stat = 6 or stat = 7 or stat = 8
         user_deleted = @true
         set.status stat

      case 1
         set.status stat
   end case

return user_deleted
end

* END-CODE
